  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies remain fairly hazy and smoky due\nto the active wildfires across the western United States. Look for\nthe smoke to continue through the overnight with minor impacts to\nvisibility and air quality. Winds will be on the light side with\novernight lows in the 50s. No precipitation is expected.\n\nWednesday through Friday, the main story continues to be the\nwarming temperatures through the mid-week period. This is due to a\nfairly strong upper level ridge that will undergo some\nstrengthening as it retrogrades west in the coming day or two. As\na result, high temperatures will climb back into the triple digits\nin some locales on Wednesday with an expansion of those triple \ndigit readings to many locations on Thursday. Some record \ntemperatures are sure to be broken during the upcoming heatwave. \nDue to the intensity and duration of the heat, have issued extreme\nheat watches for all of northern and central Nevada from Thursday\nafternoon to Sunday evening. High temperatures will be in the 90s\nWednesday afternoon, rising into the upper 90s to low 100s on \nThursday and Friday. Overnight low temperatures will be in the 50s\nand 60s for the period. Winds will generally remain light though \nsome occasional gusts to 20 mph are expected in the afternoon.\n\nSaturday and Sunday, the heat is expected to continue into the\nupcoming weekend, though model simulations are showing slight\nincreases in mid-level moisture. As the upper ridge shifts east \nto the four-corners region, look for this moisture to slowly make \nits way into central Nevada. A low probability of isolated dry \nthunderstorms continues across portions of central Nevada through \nthe weekend. Confidence remains low on the timing and coverage of \nstorms and may complicate current fire fighting efforts in central\nNevada. This scenario will need to be watched for possible \nheadline fire weather products. Afternoon high temperatures will \ncontinue to be hot with readings in the upper 90s to low 100s. \nOvernight lows will be in the 50s and 60s. One additional area of \nconcern fire weather wise is across northern Nevada, where breezy \nwinds will combine with low relative humidity values to bring \nelevated fire weather conditions. While wind speeds are slightly \nbelow RFW criteria at this time, future model runs will need to be\nscrutinized for Fire Weather product potential.\n\nMonday continues to look warm across the north with readings in\nthe upper 90s to low 100s remaining, though slightly cooler\ntemperatures in the low to mid 90s may bring some relief to the\nhot temperatures for central Nevada.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence exists in \ncontinued smoky conditions affecting the area through the near\nterm. High confidence also continues with hot temperatures \nreturning for the mid-week period, prompting an extreme heat \nwatch for all locations from Thursday through the weekend. \nModerate confidence exists for elevated fire weather conditions \nacross Northern NV late this week. Low confidence in the timing \nand placement for isolated dry thunderstorms continue for the \nweekend period across central Nevada. Grid edits were not needed \nfor NBM output at this time.",
